WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.330
This is a Wendover Productions video made
possible by Audible.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.760
So I recently went down the rabbit hole of
looking at old flight schedules.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.269
Airlines used to publish these physical brochures
with their fares and flight times, and when

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:18.950
looking at this American Airlines one from
1967 I noticed something interesting.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:25.660
This flight between New York and LA was scheduled
as 5 hours and 43 minutes long—that didn’t

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.660
seem right.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.330
American flight 3 was scheduled to leave JFK
airport everyday at 12 noon and arrive at

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.860
LAX at 2:43 pm—there’s 3 hours time difference.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:41.210
It turns out that nowadays American Airlines
flight 3 still leaves JFK daily at noon, but

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:47.920
the difference is that today, flight 3 is
scheduled to arrive at LAX at 3:27 pm—44

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.510
minutes later than in 1967.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.620
This has happened across the board—almost
every flight today takes longer than it did

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.350
back in the 60s.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.690
In general the actual flight times—the time
in the air—is the same but with all the

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:07.390
congestion and delays at airports the scheduled
times now account for things going wrong.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.950
What this means though is that, overall, flying
has slowed down.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:17.700
In 1967 we hadn’t been to the moon and computers
looked like this but we were flying everywhere

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.290
just as fast or even faster than we do today.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:26.690
What happened that caused this immense lack
of progress in the last 50 years?

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:33.510
There are three main types of aircraft engine—the
turboprop, turbofan, and turbojet—and each

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.070
of them has a range of speeds when they're
most efficient.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:41.630
The turboprop is the kind of engine you see
on most propeller aircraft.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.400
Almost all of the thrust with turboprop engines
comes from the propeller.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.030
The turbine which spins the propeller does
intake and speed up some air, but the exhaust

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:55.200
air is not at a very high speed so it only
accounts for less than 10% of the overall

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.200
thrust.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.920
These engines are generally inexpensive both
to buy and operate so a lot of smaller commuter

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.340
planes use turboprop engines.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.770
Of course there’s a trade-off—they’re
not as fast.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:12.260
They’re most efficient between about 325
and 375 mph.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.600
Any faster than that its better to use a turbofan.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.819
Now, these are the engines that you see everywhere.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:22.200
Almost every commercial aircraft is turbofan
driven.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.290
With turbofans, the air is initially sped
up by a fan—that’s what you see when you

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.040
look at an engine from the front.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.480
Then, some of the air goes into the interior
combustion chamber where the actual turbine

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.810
that drives the fan is and the rest of it
goes around the turbine.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.550
While air that bypasses the turbine is also
sped up, the majority of the thrust comes

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.540
from the air that passes through the turbine.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:48.920
Turbofans are most efficient at the speeds
you see most aircraft fly today—400-620

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.160
mph.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:56.709
If you want to go supersonic—above 767 mph—you
need a turbojet.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:03:01.680
Turbojets are very similar to turbofans except
all the air goes through the turbine—no

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.100
air is bypassed.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.620
This lets them achieve extremely high speeds
but they also require an immense amount of

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.620
fuel.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.500
These engines are really only efficient between
about 1,300-1,400 mph.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.180
What really determine the efficiency of engines
is something called the bypass ratio.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:24.220
That’s the ratio of the amount of air that
passes through the bypass duct to the amount

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.269
that passes through the engine core.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:31.569
The thing is, it really doesn’t take that
much more energy to spin a larger fan—what

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:35.500
requires a lot more fuel is to put more air
through the engine core.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.319
That means that engines that accelerate more
air through the bypass duct can get more thrust

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:46.680
for the same amount of energy so, as a rule,
the higher the bypass ratio the more efficient

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.700
the engine.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.550
Take a look at this General Electric GEnx
engine.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:57.519
This is a relatively new super-efficient engine
used on both the 787 Dreamliner and the 747-8i.

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:01.400
You can see the fan is much larger than the
turbine itself.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:07.280
That’s because this engine has a bypass
ratio of 10:1—10 times more air goes around

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.020
the turbine than through it.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:14.680
Compare that to the CFM International CFM56—an
older and less efficient engine.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.969
You can see there’s much less fan relative
to the turbine so this engine only has a bypass

00:04:19.969 --> 00:04:24.430
ratio of 5.9:1, but that’s still considered
to be high.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:29.009
The difference is most striking when comparing
either of those engines to the Pratt &amp; Whitney

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:30.210
JT8D.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:36.330
This engine has a bypass ratio of only .96:1
so it’s astoundingly inefficient but its

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:42.020
still significantly more efficient than the
Rolls-Royce/Snecma Olympus 593.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.289
This engine is a turbojet.

00:04:44.289 --> 00:04:49.680
As I mentioned, these drive all the air the
fan picks up through the turbine so that means

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.540
no air is bypassed.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:58.319
Therefore, it has a bypass ratio of 0:1 and
is what’s known as a zero-bypass engine.

00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:02.839
Since 100% of the air passing through the
engine goes through the turbine, the fuel

00:05:02.839 --> 00:05:10.050
consumption is significantly higher than that
of the GEnx, CFM56, or even the JT8D.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:18.289
The Concorde, using the zero-bypass Rolls-Royce/Snecma
Olympus 593, burned 46.85 pounds of fuel per

00:05:18.289 --> 00:05:24.879
mile flown, while the 787 Dreamliner, using
the 10:1 bypass ratio General Electric GEnx,

00:05:24.879 --> 00:05:31.300
uses 18.7 pounds per mile but the Concorde
was a tiny airplane even compared to the modestly

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.969
sized 787.

00:05:32.969 --> 00:05:39.189
It seated only 100 passengers compared to
291 on the Dreamliner.

00:05:39.189 --> 00:05:44.080
That means that the per-person fuel economy
on the Concorde was just about 14 miles per

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.369
gallon compared to 104 miles per gallon on
the Dreamliner.

00:05:48.369 --> 00:05:53.059
In the end, Air France and British Airways,
the only two Concorde operators, could not

00:05:53.059 --> 00:05:55.310
afford to keep flying the plane.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:06:00.020
Less than 1/3 of the seats were actually occupied
by paying customers.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:04.360
Others were filled by those using miles or
those upgraded from first class on normal

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.360
flights.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:12.189
After all, it cost at a minimum $7,500 of
today’s dollars to fly one way from London

00:06:12.189 --> 00:06:18.090
to New York in those three hours and that
was to fly in seats that looked like this—not

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.169
all that different than the economy seats
of today.

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:25.819
When the Concorde started flying, first class
on other planes looked like this.

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:30.539
While nice, these seats were just larger economy
class seats and it wasn’t incredibly easy

00:06:30.539 --> 00:06:31.779
to sleep in them.

00:06:31.779 --> 00:06:37.159
By the time the Concorde stopped flying in
2003, first class looked like this and the

00:06:37.159 --> 00:06:39.509
seats went fully flat into a bed.

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:45.189
Many chose to spend a little less to pass
7 hours in this rather than spending 3 hours

00:06:45.189 --> 00:06:47.069
in these cramped seats.

00:06:47.069 --> 00:06:51.890
British Airways even introduced the first
fully-flat business class seat in 2000 so

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:56.839
for significantly less money than the Concorde,
travelers could cross the Atlantic sleeping

00:06:56.839 --> 00:06:58.179
horizontally.

00:06:58.179 --> 00:07:00.599
This just wasn’t luxury anymore.

00:07:00.599 --> 00:07:05.449
The whole idea of the Concorde was to create
the most efficient way to cross the Atlantic

00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:10.159
for the business traveller, but with fully-flat
beds, those traveling towards Europe could

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:15.689
leave the US in the evening, get their nights
sleep on the plane and wake up in Europe—essentially

00:07:15.689 --> 00:07:17.479
wasting no time.

00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:22.889
No longer luxurious or efficient, the Concorde
flew its final commercial fight on October

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:28.319
24, 2003 thereby ending the era of commercial
supersonic flight.

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:32.930
Here’s the thing about flying—speed really
doesn’t matter to airlines.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.990
It really only exists as a selling point for
the consumer.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:40.759
The cost of the airplane is a relatively small
part of the overall cost to fly so you’ll

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:44.759
never see an airline fly faster so they can
use their planes more.

00:07:44.759 --> 00:07:50.039
Planes lifespans are generally rated in cycles—the
number of times the plane takes off and lands.

00:07:50.039 --> 00:07:54.959
The Dreamliner is rated for 44,000 cycles
and has a list price, which is often much

00:07:54.959 --> 00:08:00.020
higher than the actual sale price, of $224.6
million dollars.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:05.300
That means that the cost of the airplane per
flight is barely over $5,000 while the fuel

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.979
cost for a flight from New York to London
is well over $15,000.

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:15.270
Therefore, airlines always just fly their
airplanes at the most fuel efficient speed.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:21.259
It turns out that that speed is just about
always between 500-550 miles per hour.

00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:27.159
What’s weird about this is that its well
below the speed of sound—767 miles per hour.

00:08:27.159 --> 00:08:30.439
Why don’t planes fly just below the speed
of sound?

00:08:30.439 --> 00:08:34.500
Well, this graph shows the drag on airplanes
at different speeds.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.409
Between Mach 0.8 and mach 1.2 is what’s
known as the transonic range.

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:44.930
At these speeds, the airflow around an airplane
is not fully subsonic or supersonic.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:48.660
Essentially, some air is subsonic and some
is supersonic.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:54.750
So beginning at mach 0.8, some airflow becomes
supersonic which increases drag exponentially

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:59.441
and destabilizes the aircraft so its actually
quite dangerous to fly right around the speed

00:08:59.441 --> 00:09:03.129
of sound—you either have to fly well above
or below.

00:09:03.129 --> 00:09:06.940
You can actually see when an airplane is flying
transonic.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:10.980
Its hard to see, but there are these lines
that look like scratches on the camera lens

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.459
but actually are mini supersonic shock waves.

00:09:14.459 --> 00:09:20.020
Because of the disturbances in the airflow,
flying between mach .8 and mach 1.2 actually

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:26.870
requires more fuel than flying above mach
1.2 so that’s why we have this number—613.8

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:32.560
mph—that’s the speed limit for commercially
viable subsonic jets.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.500
While supersonic flight and crossing the Atlantic
in 3 hours is flashy and exciting, what’s

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:43.740
truly impressive is hopping the pond for $100
or $200 on an airline that’s actually making

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.290
a profit, and that’s becoming more of a
reality today.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:53.750
With current speeds, airplanes are able to
fly anywhere on earth in 24 hours and that’s

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:55.680
fast enough for almost everyone.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:01.660
The barrier to travel for most people is cost,
not speed, so manufacturers and airlines will

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:06.860
continue to focus their efforts on driving
down the cost of travel, not the time.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:13.540
In the end, time is the enemy of the privileged
few, cost is the enemy of the masses.

